    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$2.84 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for chicken inasal with rice. Filipino barbecue plates run $12 to $15 at the restaurants that serve them, and the closest one might be a state away. Your version is 14 servings deep.",
    "shop_smart":  ["Chicken thighs are the whole main event at 7 pounds for the batch. Thighs take a hard sear and a sweet-sour marinade far better than breasts, and cost less doing it.","The achiote paste looks pricey per jar, but this batch only uses about a third of one and the jar keeps for months. It is the color and earthiness of the dish; do not skip it.","White vinegar, lime juice, and brown sugar make a marinade for pocket change. Bottled barbecue marinades cost several times as much and would be wrong for this dish anyway."],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Make the marinade: whisk the white vinegar, lime juice, lemongrass paste, garlic, ginger, brown sugar, salt, and black pepper.",
                    "Cut the chicken thighs into large pieces, toss with the marinade, and refrigerate at least 1 hour, up to overnight.",
                    "Cook the rice: 3.75 cups dry rice in a rice cooker or covered pot.",
                    "Make the annatto butter: melt the butter with the achiote paste over low heat, mashing until the butter turns deep orange.",
                    "Grill or sear the chicken over high heat, 5 to 6 minutes per side, basting generously with the annatto butter, until charred at the edges and cooked through to 165 F.",
                    "Brush the cooked chicken with any remaining baste (bring leftover baste to a brief boil first if it touched raw chicken).",
                    "Portion rice into 14 containers and top with the basted chicken."
                ],
